================================================================================
RiQuA: Rich Quotation Annotation Corpus
Version 0.1
Institute for Natural Language Processing
University of Stuttgart
Stuttgart, Germany
================================================================================
RiQuA is a corpus of literary texts annotated for quotations and their
interpersonal structures. Quotations, direct and indirect, are marked, and for
each quote, cues, speakers and addressees are identified where applicable.

The individual annotations from both annotators are provided, along with the
final, merged annotations.  The merged annotations are generally of higher
quality, and should be used in most circumstances.

The documents in the corpus mostly correspond to those  in the Columbia
Quoted Speech Attribution Corpus (Elson & McKeown 2010):

austen_emma_1         Jane Austen, Emma, passage 1 of 3
austen_emma_2         Jane Austen, Emma, passage 2 of 3
austen_emma_3         Jane Austen, Emma, passage 3 of 3
chekhov_lady          Anton Chekhov, The Lady with the Dog
chekhov_monk          Anton Chekhov, The Black Monk
chekhov_steppe        Anton Chekhov, The Steppe
dickens_xmas          Charles Dickens, A Christmas Carol
doyle_boscombe        Sir Arthur Conan Doyle, The Boscombe Valley Mystery
doyle_identity        Sir Arthur Conan Doyle, A Case of Identity
doyle_league          Sir Arthur Conan Doyle, The Red Headed League
doyle_scandal         Sir Arthur Conan Doyle, A Scandal in Bohemia
flaubert_bovary_1     Gustave Flaubert, Madame Bovary, passage 1 of 2
flaubert_bovary_2     Gustave Flaubert, Madame Bovary, passage 2 of 2
twain_sawyer_1        Mark Twain, The Adventures of Tom Sawyer, passage 1 of 2
twain_sawyer_2        Mark Twain, The Adventures of Tom Sawyer, passage 2 of 2

All documents are represented in BRAT format, with the raw text in the .txt files
and the annotations in the corresponding .ann files

